00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
hello everyone welcome to another coffee  break here on my channel firm learning  today i want to talk about the terrible  events in ukraine and how this affects  consulting firms and especially the  russian business of the consulting firms  around the world after russia invading  the ukraine you see many organizations  around the world to speak up and take  actions by for instance stopping  business in russia though is this the  case for the consulting firms as well  and what are maybe differences among the  different firms and how they react to  the russian business and of course what  does this imply for the employees  working in these russian organizations  so this is what i want to talk about  today in this video i will donate all  the youtube ad revenue that i receive  from this video to the unhcr which is  the un agency mandated to aid and  protect refugees around the world  they're currently also active in ukraine  so if you watch the video we'll directly  contribute to that if you watch the ads  and watch until the very end thank you  very much for your support on this so in  order to put together this video i did  some desk research read lots of press  statements also statements on linkedin  the many consulting firms and the  leaders of these firms published and i  reached out to you on instagram asking  for your first hand impressions first  and insider knowledge on what is going  on in all these consulting firms and  what they're doing with their russian  organization so thanks so much for all  the messages that i did receive but do  want to say is a disclaimer that first  the situation is dynamically evolving so  potentially the information i'm sharing  right now is not accurate anymore in a  couple of days or a couple of weeks and  second because lots of the information  i'm sharing in this video is from you  from the community i've no direct way of  verifying it though if any of you proves  me wrong that anything i say as of today  is not accurate i will include a pinned  post in the comment section with any  clarifications or any amendments or  corrections of what i'm saying so let's  get started and first of all i think  it's fair to say that the whole  situation did leave a mark on the  consulting industry lots of firms did  take action the moment will go firm by  firm for many of the major firms what  exactly they did they even was a  statement from alexander kravchenko  which was the managing director of  mckinsey in ukraine who released a post  on linkedin saying that the russian  actions were a military crime so from my  experience such a candid political  statement is rather rare from the  leadership of any big consulting firm as  far as i know that person also uttered  that as a private person not necessarily  as an official spokesperson for the firm  but still this reaction is remarkable  and surely nothing like everyday  business what's going on right now let's  look into individual reforms and let's  start with the mbb strategy consulting  firms for mckinsey bcg and bain shortly  after the news of the invasion mckinsey  released a statement saying that they  will not conduct any work for the  russian government and then a couple of  days later they extended that to not  accepting any new work for any russian  client whatsoever and rather similar  statements were released by the  leadership of boston consulting group  and of bain so what exactly does this  mean for the staff of these  organizations working in russia once  again i want to reiterate that all of  this information was collected from the  community i wasn't able to verify any of  it feel free to leave a comment if you  know more and apparently for mckinsey  the situation is that many colleagues  got transferred to the kazakhstan office  and from there they will conduct lion  work for especially middle eastern  clients but then also other  international projects and i know from  at least one instance where an offer  that a candidate received during the  interview process was postponed in  boston consulting group they seem to be  in the middle of the process of  reallocating consultants to other  offices so the process seems to be more  top down not necessarily only focus on  one office but more depending on the  individual situation of the consultants  and here i've been told that especially  for some juniors this process poses some  issues so far it doesn't seem to be  clear what will happen to the colleagues  who are in the end not able to make this  switch to make this transition to  another office bain seems to have  transferred a good part of their  consultants to the dubai office and then  helping them to get staffed on  international projects also projects in  the middle east as with the other firms  it seems to be an ongoing process but i  have been told by sources from all of  these firms that so far there was no  layoff directly related to the situation  in ukraine next is oliver wyman and  while the oliver wyman leadership stated  that they don't want to conduct any more  client work in russia there was a press  release from marsh mclennan which is the  company that owns oliver wyman that said  we intend to transfer ownership of our  russian businesses to local management  will operate independently in the  russian market so what this means is and  i got this confirmed from some local  sources that apparently they're trying  to do is that they're asking the  partners the local partners in russia to  buy out the russian organization of  oliver wyman and then potentially  continue operating it under a different  name now in this video i don't want to  judge these actions i trust this might  be good news to the russian employees as  maybe likely they will not lose the job  or might not need to move to other  countries other regions other offices i  also understand though that so far  there's no definite agreement on this  matter similar to the others kearney  also released a statement that they want  to stop any work for russian clients but  do want to keep the offices still open  so far was frankly not able to collect  any insider information from any russian  employee at kearney right now so if you  have anything to share on that please  leave a comment in the comment section  next is olamberger they behaving very  much in line with the other companies so  they're stopping any client activities  in russia to my knowledge leaving the  offices open for now they are relocating  colleagues mainly to the middle east and  also some other international offices in  helping them to get staff to  international projects from there  accenture arguably took the most drastic  measures from the companies on this list  they completely discontinued all  operations in russia and laid off all of  their apparently 2  300 people working in that country  overall the whole organization was laid  off and the severance offer that these  people receive this at least was i was  told is that they got three monthly  salaries as a severance payment let's  next talk about the big four so this is  pwc kpmg e y and deloitte what's first  important to understand is that we talk  about the big four firms that due to  legal regulations also due to their  audit business they are structured in  local legally independent subsidiaries  so there is a legal entity for the  russian business for each of these four  companies and what all of these four  firms announced is that they want to  stop the affiliation of the russian  entity from the global network of any of  these four firms so what this means is  that the entity that was for instance  called pwc russia in the future cannot  call itself pwc anymore but that entity  still exists and can still continue its  operation and i trust this is what is  happening for all the four big four  companies so in all fairness given that  these are legally independent entities  it probably was more or less difficult  maybe even impossible than for the  mother organization to completely stop  the operations in this country of course  i'm not a legal expert on these issues  the result surely is that these entities  will continue its operations likely  under a new name in the future and i  received some details from an internal  memo of one of these russian  organizations from one of these firms  and in this memo russian leadership  stated that they actually see the  situation from now on positively because  now there will be less competition from  international firms and on top they will  also get access to lots of talent from  other consulting firms staff that was  laid off or did not want to relocate to  any other location those of course are  people who will likely maybe want to  work for any of these firms going  forward so interesting situation let me  know what you think about this in the  comments hope this served as an  informative overview of the situation of  consulting firms in russia if you took  any value out of this video is always  destroy the like button for the youtube  algorithm and also subscribe to stay up  to date on my content big big thanks to  all the members of this channel  supporting my work once again i will  donate all ad revenue from this video to  the un refugee agency my name is  heinrich i release weekly videos every  saturday thank you all for watching  until next week and goodbye

